1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,006
The solution to case PORTFLOW, and everything to check before you submit.

2
00:00:06,010 --> 00:00:14,766
Our answer: option B, Integrated flow package: shared visibility, appointments, AI-assisted yard planning.

3
00:00:14,766 --> 00:00:24,514
It delivers 21.7 percent against a target of 19.5 percent, for 9.00 million dollars of a 10.00 million dollars budget.

4
00:00:25,510 --> 00:00:27,277
Option B is recommended.

5
00:00:27,277 --> 00:00:33,094
It is both the cheapest compliant option and the best value: a dominant choice.

6
00:00:34,090 --> 00:00:45,400
The package has 7 initiatives for 9.00 million dollars upfront, 1.30 million dollars a year to run, and 6.40 million dollars a year of savings.

7
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:53,626
Using the case formula, the five-year net financial impact is minus 16.50 million dollars, a net saving.

8
00:00:54,630 --> 00:01:23,406
Operations & Safety: Storage: dwell 6.0 to 4.7 days keeps year-5 utilization near 83% with no new land Supply Chain: Lines, trucks, rail and customers share one release and ETA status Organizational Change Management: Staff co-design rules before tools arrive; training in paid time with super-users per shift AI & Automation: AI recommends stacking and overnight moves; planners approve; safety rules locked

9
00:01:24,410 --> 00:01:35,258
The economics: a run-rate benefit of 6.40 million dollars a year, net present value of 6.49 million dollars, and payback in 32 months.

10
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:37,333
Now stress it.

11
00:01:37,333 --> 00:01:46,381
If performance falls fifteen percent and costs rise twenty, the gain is 18.45 percent and NPV is 620 thousand dollars.

12
00:01:46,381 --> 00:01:51,595
The target would be missed, which is why the pilot gate comes first.

13
00:01:51,595 --> 00:01:57,116
The most sensitive driver is annual benefit: achieved gain x unit value.

14
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:00,929
Days 1 to 30: Validate & pilot.

15
00:02:00,929 --> 00:02:05,369
Confirm baseline data and the binding constraint.

16
00:02:05,369 --> 00:02:08,088
Days 31 to 60: Prove & decide.

17
00:02:08,088 --> 00:02:15,247
Pilot gate: Slot adherence at least 80% on pilot lanes and no safety incidents.

18
00:02:15,247 --> 00:02:18,056
Days 61 to 90: Scale & control.

19
00:02:18,056 --> 00:02:22,768
Begin scale appointments, positioning and ai advice.

20
00:02:23,770 --> 00:02:37,197
The three risks to manage: Truckers do not adopt appointments, mitigated by slot rules co-designed with trucking firms, grace windows, priority lanes for on-time trucks, a walk-in lane kept during transition.

21
00:02:37,197 --> 00:02:43,975
Dwell does not fall enough, mitigated by free-time tariff, release alerts, empties evacuation agreements.

22
00:02:43,975 --> 00:02:53,722
Cutover disrupts live operations, mitigated by lane-by-lane and block-by-block rollout, parallel running, go-lives outside vessel peaks, rollback plan.

23
00:02:54,720 --> 00:02:59,091
Question: Why not just build on the 15-acre parcel?

24
00:02:59,091 --> 00:03:06,204
Answer: Space alone fails the objective: 25,344 TEU / 28,000 TEU = 90.5% at year 5.

25
00:03:06,204 --> 00:03:13,660
It costs about $14.5M, over the $10M budget, and must be phased around live operations.

26
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:19,230
Cutting dwell to 4.7 days reaches about 83% on today's footprint.

27
00:03:19,230 --> 00:03:25,143
We keep the parcel as a phase-2 option if dwell stays above 5.6 days.

28
00:03:25,143 --> 00:03:30,285
Question: What if dwell does not fall as much as you assume?

29
00:03:30,285 --> 00:03:37,655
Answer: The threshold is explicit: dwell at or below 4.83 days meets 85% without land.

30
00:03:37,655 --> 00:03:47,768
If dwell is above 5.3 days at month 9 (our gate trigger), we tighten the free-time tariff and start the parcel design.

31
00:03:47,768 --> 00:03:54,024
With dwell at 5.6 days or less, the parcel would then meet the objective.

32
00:03:55,020 --> 00:03:57,775
Before you submit, run the checklist.

33
00:03:57,775 --> 00:03:59,636
10 of 11 items are ready.

34
00:03:59,636 --> 00:04:03,285
Still open: assumptions validated with real data.

35
00:04:03,285 --> 00:04:10,284
Replace the assumptions with real data if you can, or state them clearly on an appendix slide.

36
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:16,606
Lead with the answer, prove it with three numbers, and own the risks.

37
00:04:16,606 --> 00:04:17,300
Option B.

38
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:18,072
Good luck.
